akaean wrote:

 > I am of the opinion that Ranger and Knight are actually the easiest
 > classes to go out and ascend with. [a really good and very detailed
 > explanation snipped]

That's what I mean with "adjust to the advantages a role offers".

I used to play knights like valkyries and samurais. I used the long
sword, dipped for Excalibur, later two-weaponed. I found the horse
annoying for some reason, so I never rode. This approach works, but
does not utilize a knight's full potential. And it made them quite
boring frankly, so I hardly played them.

Eventually, I tried to play them "the right way". At one point, when
I rode a warhorse and jousted monsters left and right, I asked myself
why I didn't play like that earlier. Because it was *fun*, too.

Now, a knight riding (flying) a pet ki-rin and using a +7 lance on
monsters is one of the best NetHack experiences I ever had.

 > So while it might be easier for a beginer to start playing with a
 > Valk or a Barbarian, what I'm essentially arguing is that by the
 > time a player has the skill to potentially pull off an ascention,
 > Ranger or Knight will give the starting player a better chance of
 > pulling it off - because their starting kits have more tactical
 > flexibilty built into them.

Beginners would benefit from those role advantages as well. If they
would know about them. And if they would play these roles in the first
place. Instead they mostly play valkyries or barbarians because they
hear everywhere that these are the easiest.

 > In Slash'EM... Rogue is by far the easiest class, strong starting
 > gear, the best weapon in the game as a guarenteed first sacrafice
 > gift (bat from hell makes grayswandir pale with its flat +20
 > damage), and a variety of useful techniques for offing monsters in
 > one hit.

In vanilla, rogues enjoy back-stab damage. Once you've killed a purple
L or minotaur with a single volley of daggers, you wonder why you have
two-weaponed all the time. At least I did #-).

 > Of course, early game is the most difficult part of ascending and
 > for that reason I wouldn't classify wizard as being easy (unless of
 > course you roll like a fiend for starting inventory...)

In my experience, the most valuable assets for wizards are the ones
that are guaranteed. The quarterstaff does okay damage until you get
Magicbane (only melee weapon a wizard needs). Force bolt suffices for
the rest. The CoMR nullifies wands of death and magic missile and
prevents things like lycanthropy from were-monsters. Later, it turns
polytraps into pet-upgraders.

Of the variable things a wizard can start with, only a magic marker
stands out. Because with it and maxed luck, you can write all scrolls
and spellbooks you want which usually results in SDSM, spellbooks of
magic missile and identify, and enchanted weapons/armor.

Other potential items can be useful, of course, but you can generally
do without them.

